<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Is each vector a unit vector?</p><div><m>\begin{pmatrix} \displaystyle{-1} \\ \displaystyle{0} \\ \displaystyle{1} \end{pmatrix}</m>: [ a unit vector | not a unit vector ]  </div><div><m>\begin{pmatrix} \displaystyle{-\frac{2}{3}} \\ \displaystyle{-\frac{1}{3}} \\ \displaystyle{\frac{2}{3}} \end{pmatrix}</m>: [ a unit vector | not a unit vector ]  </div><div><m>\begin{pmatrix} \displaystyle{\frac{1}{5}} \\ \displaystyle{\frac{3}{10}} \\ \displaystyle{\frac{1}{2}} \end{pmatrix}</m>: [ a unit vector | not a unit vector ]  </div><div><m>\begin{pmatrix} \displaystyle{\frac{3}{5}} \\ \displaystyle{0} \\ \displaystyle{-\frac{4}{5}} \end{pmatrix}</m>: [ a unit vector | not a unit vector ]  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>A unit vector has magnitude 1, so <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1</m>. Adding the components is not the test.</p><ul><li><m>\begin{pmatrix} \displaystyle{-1} \\ \displaystyle{0} \\ \displaystyle{1} \end{pmatrix}</m>: <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = {2}</m>, so it is not a unit vector.</li><li><m>\begin{pmatrix} \displaystyle{-\frac{2}{3}} \\ \displaystyle{-\frac{1}{3}} \\ \displaystyle{\frac{2}{3}} \end{pmatrix}</m>: <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = {1}</m>, so it is a unit vector.</li><li><m>\begin{pmatrix} \displaystyle{\frac{1}{5}} \\ \displaystyle{\frac{3}{10}} \\ \displaystyle{\frac{1}{2}} \end{pmatrix}</m>: <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = {\frac{19}{50}}</m>, so it is not a unit vector.</li><li><m>\begin{pmatrix} \displaystyle{\frac{3}{5}} \\ \displaystyle{0} \\ \displaystyle{-\frac{4}{5}} \end{pmatrix}</m>: <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = {1}</m>, so it is a unit vector.</li></ul></p></solution>
    <answer><p>not a unit vector</p></answer>
    <answer><p>a unit vector</p></answer>
    <answer><p>not a unit vector</p></answer>
    <answer><p>a unit vector</p></answer>
</stack-static>